{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And you, take for yourself prime spices, mor deror.</b> In this passage He commanded concerning the making of the oil with five kinds, and they are mor, cinnamon, cane, cassia, and olive oil; and He commanded to anoint with it the priests, Aaron and his sons, and the Tabernacle and all its vessels, and forbade making its likeness, and called it 'holy' — this is what it says: 'anointing oil, holy.' The commentators and Rambam agreed that mor deror is almusk, and so too is the opinion of our master Saadiah Gaon, of blessed memory." "<b>And it is known</b> that the musk is blood that gathers in the throat of a creature known in the land of India, resembling a deer; and in summertime, in the season of great heat, when she walks among the trees, she rubs against that and the blood comes out congealed, and people gather it from among the grasses. And thus Scripture used the language of gathering concerning it (Song of Songs 5:1): 'אריתי מורי'; and it says 'deror' to signify that it is clean and free of admixtures and adulteration." "<b>But</b> Ramban wrote in his commentary that the word 'deror' does not refer back to the mor but to that creature resembling a deer — that is, that the mor is taken from it while it walks freely among the spice-beds, and it delights as it pleases; for if it is trapped and stands in human possession, it does not produce from it but a little, and that is not fragrant either. And according to this it is the opinion of the commentators that it is almusk." "<b>But</b> it is impossible to explain thus, that mor is almusk, from what they said in the Midrash: 'צרור המור דודי לי — this is Abraham. As this mor is the head of all kinds of spices, so Abraham is the head of all the righteous. As this mor gives off fragrance only through fire, so Abraham's name was not known until he was cast into the fiery furnace. As this mor — whoever gathers it, his hands become bitter, so Abraham would cleanse and afflict himself with sufferings.' And so this is proof that the mor is not musk, for musk gives off fragrance without fire. Therefore he, of blessed memory, explained that the mor is not musk but is the substance called in the Arabic tongue lamur, and in the Roman tongue mira, and in the Persian tongue amranun, and so all the languages agree upon it. And this is what they say in the Midrash: 'whoever gathers it, his hands become bitter' — for it is bitter as wormwood. And what they said, that it is the head of all kinds of spices — that means its mention is at their head, and one should not say it is the head of them all in rank. And it says 'deror' because it is constantly adulterated; and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'Things that are impossible to detect, such as mixing water into wine, komus into mor' — komus istzamg in Arabic, and it resembles mira; therefore it says it is clean of adulteration and admixture. And the reason it mentions this first among the spices is that in burning it is superior to all of them, and the cloud of its smoke rises; and from this we say in the Scroll of Esther, 'six months with oil of mor,' for from it they extract oil just as they extract from the musk-cat and other plants — end of quotation. And from appearances this is not necessary, for the mor here may be the musk according to the opinion of the Geonim, and the 'צרור המור' of Scripture would be that which is called mira, and both this and that would be the head of all kinds of spices: this one is the head of all kinds of spices for burning and for going up upon the fire, and that one too is the head of all other spices for giving off fragrance without fire; and so it is written (Psalms 45:9): 'מור ואהלות קציעות כל בגדותיך.'" "<b>Half of it, two hundred and fifty.</b> It is known that half of 500 is 250; and why did he elaborate to say 'fifty and two hundred'? But our Rabbis, of blessed memory, received that the weight of the cinnamon is 250 like the mor deror, except that the mor deror was weighed 250 all at once, whereas the cinnamon — a decree of Scripture — is to be weighed twice, 250 each time, in order to determine it in two determinations, for one does not weigh item for item; and so it is taught in Kereitot."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:05:55.756147910Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}